\chapter{Stellar Hydrostatic Engine}
\label{chap:stellar}

\section{Introduction}

This chapter describes the stellar hydrostatic equilibrium engine within
the Blackwell Framework, modeling self-gravitating, radiative stars under
the unified Hamiltonian $H_{\star}$.

\section{Governing Equations}

\subsection{Hydrostatic Equilibrium}
\begin{equation}
\frac{dP(r)}{dr}
=
-\frac{G M(r)\rho(r)}{r^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Mass Conservation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{dM(r)}{dr}
=
4\pi r^{2}\rho(r).
\end{equation}

\subsection{Energy Generation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{dL(r)}{dr}
=
4\pi r^{2}\rho(r)\varepsilon(\rho,T,X_i).
\end{equation}

\subsection{Radiative Transport}
\begin{equation}
\frac{dT(r)}{dr}
=
-\frac{3\kappa(r)\rho(r)}{16\pi a c}
\frac{L(r)}{T^{3}(r) r^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\section{Equation of State and Opacity}

\begin{equation}
P(r) = P_{\text{gas}}(\rho,T) + P_{\text{rad}}(T),
\end{equation}
with opacity $\kappa(r)$ drawn from stellar grids.

\section{Integration Under Encrypted Hamiltonian}

The stellar engine evolves under $H_{\star}$ while respecting the
Blackwell Axioms and Theorem~\ref{thm:blackwell}, ensuring stability and
energy conservation.

